      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246974/assets/e006f41f3c8a0201623c88bd4f3738bea05258690c8a0adef297769341a3f490.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m∠1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Consider $∠2$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246974/assets/2bca55a68d4a0695c73f82ca82d5fae386d9b8d57b058bc1cf694bee902cff25.png)\n\nNotice that $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${42}^{∘}$ are alternate exterior angles formed when the transversal $u$ cuts the parallel lines $r$ and $s.$ Hence, \n\n$$\nm∠2={42}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that the angle determined by $∠1$ and $∠2$ and the angle whose measure is ${108}^{∘}$ are corresponding angles formed when the transversal $v$ cuts the parallel lines $s$ and $t.$ Hence, \n\n$$\nm∠1+m∠2={108}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $m∠2$ into the equation, we can find $m∠1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠1+m∠2 & ={108}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1+{42}^{∘} & ={108}^{∘} \\\\ m∠1 & ={66}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
